  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000116824,
      "SummaryText": "RISK MANAGEMENT PLAN - POLYCOAT PRODUCTS - Santa Fe Springs Facility\n\nEXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\nPolycoat Products, a division of American Polymers Corporation, has established a Risk Management Plan according to EPA's regulations (40 CFR Part 68) for the TDI Storage and Transfer System to enhance its already existing safety programs.  Polycoat Products is extremely cautious in the handling of all chemicals and is very diligent about safety for its employees, visitors and its neighbors.  Polycoat Products staff is well trained and utilizes modern equipment to provide safeguards.  The TDI Transfer System, that contains the regulated substance Toluene Di-Isocyanate (TDI), is used for supplying TDI to the polymer production reactors in the process area.  \nAccidental Release Prevention and Emergency Response Policies\nPolycoat Products has an emergency response plan in place to handle potential emergency situations at this facility.  This plan was designed to meet the following objectives:\n1.) To save lives.\n2.) To minimize and avoid injuries.\n3.) To protect the environment.\n4.) To minimize property damage.\n\nKey elements of the Emergency Response Plan include specific steps that Polycoat Products management expects of its employees to take to protect health of the employees, visitors and the neighbors.  The Emergency Response Plan provides the response organization and notification procedures, key phone numbers and contact points, vicinity and evacuation maps, and instructions for evacuation and for actions to be taken in case of a fire, injury, earthquake and chemical spill.  The Emergency Plan is reviewed and updated periodically to ensure that it remains current.  \nStationary Source and Regulated Substance\nThe TDI Transfer System consists of two processes.\n\nProcess 1: A 6340 gallon double walled storage tank, a transfer pump, piping, valves and a bank of scrubbers that are used during the filling operation.  TDI storage tank is designed for truck and marine transport like a c"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000116824,
      "SummaryText": "ontainerized cargo.  Therefore, it includes features to enhance its ruggedness.  The tank is used as a stationary tank at this site.  All piping attached to the tank, penetrate tank boundaries from the top.  This, in addition to the double wall feature, provides an added safety against spillage of tank contents.  \nProcess 2: 6600 gallon in one twenty 55-gallon storage drums for surplus TDI. Currently there are 40 drums which the site will be increasing to 120. Hence for caution the RMP submission is being done for higher capacity. These drum are kept in the hot room#1 of the adjacent building 2 (14800).\nTDI is used in small quantities in the reactors to produce polymers.  Transfer is accomplished via a transfer pump that is controlled automatically by a flow meter.  The operator sets the desired quantity in the automatic control unit which then transfers the desired amount to a reactor. \nAccidental Release Prevention Program and Chemical-Specific Prevention Steps\nPolycoat Products has a Risk Management Program in place to manage the handling of TDI.  The program includes strict requirements for written operating procedures, personnel training, equipment maintenance, incident investigation, process hazard analysis, management of change and periodic review or audit of program elements.  Written procedures are developed as part of this program that specify the steps for every task undertaken related to the TDI system.  Management of change element addresses proper implementation of system updates to ensure that all changes are reviewed properly by knowledgeable persons, all operating procedures reflect the change, and personnel are trained on the change.   The Risk Management Program also requires a pre-startup safety review to ensure that the system is properly reviewed before it is put back into service after a major change.  In addition to the Risk Management Program, common industry standards, policies, and procedures are strictly followed.\nIn this update Polycoat"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 2,
      "FacilityID": 1000116824,
      "SummaryText": "Products is moving from Program 1 to 2 due to an accident that occurred on March 15, 2024. As a result of this update, the facility will now be complying with the prevention program level 2 instead of 1 in order to ensure enhance safety management.\n\nFive Year Accident History\nThe facility had an accident on March 15, 2024 which involved release of processed Urepol 18HV \u0096 Urethane Prepolyme. TDI- the regulated substance at site was not released but was being used to manufacture Urepol 18HV \u0096 Urethane Prepolymer chemical, hence the facility is updating the program level. The released chemical was in non-toxic form. The incident report was timely prepared for the accident and corrective measures are being performed.\n\nPlanned Changes to Improve Safety\nThe accidental release was thoroughly studied. Polycoat Products is moving to program 2 to implement enhanced risk management. The facility is taking additional steps to further public and personnel safety.  Several studies have been conducted recently to examine potential release scenarios and effectiveness of existing mitigation measures and to take additional steps to further enhance safety."
    }
  ]
